#
# Review grammar for SCIgen: generates a conference-style review of a
# generated paper. Driven by make-review.pl.
#
# The driver defines SYSNAME, PAPER_TITLE, PAPER_THING (a topic phrase taken
# from the paper's title or abstract), and PAPER_FIELD, and enables sections
# that steer the tone:
#
#   merit1 .. merit5         the Overall Merit score
#   negative                 merit 1 or 2
#   middling                 merit 3
#   positive                 merit 4 or 5
#   expertise1 .. expertise4 the Reviewer Expertise score
#   novice                   expertise 1 or 2
#   expert                   expertise 3 or 4
#
# Rules defined only inside sections must be defined in every section of
# their group, since the base grammar has no fallback for them.

#    This file is part of SCIgen.
#
#    SCIgen is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify
#    it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
#    the Free Software Foundation; either version 2 of the License, or
#    (at your option) any later version.
#
#    SCIgen is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
#    but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
#    MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
#    GNU General Public License for more details.

SCIRULES.include scirules.in

#############################################################################
####### Top level #######
#############################################################################

REV_SUMMARY=text
REV_SUMMARY+3 REV_SUM_OPEN REV_SUM_CONTEXT REV_SUM_APPROACH REV_SUM_IMPL REV_SUM_EVAL REV_SUM_CLOSE
REV_SUMMARY+2 REV_SUM_OPEN REV_SUM_APPROACH REV_SUM_APPROACH_MORE REV_SUM_IMPL REV_SUM_EVAL REV_SUM_CLOSE
REV_SUMMARY REV_SUM_OPEN REV_SUM_CONTEXT REV_SUM_APPROACH REV_SUM_APPROACH_MORE REV_SUM_EVAL REV_SUM_CLOSE

REV_COMMENTS {
REV_OPENING

REV_PROSE_CONCERN

Strengths:
REV_STRENGTHS
Weaknesses:
REV_WEAKNESSES
Detailed comments:
REV_DETAILS
REV_CLOSING
}

REV_COMMENTS {
REV_OPENING

REV_PROSE_CONCERN

Strengths:
REV_STRENGTHS
Weaknesses:
REV_WEAKNESSES
Questions for the authors:
REV_Q_ITEM
REV_Q_ITEM

Minor comments:
REV_DETAILS
REV_CLOSING
}

REV_COMMENTS {
REV_OPENING

Strengths:
REV_STRENGTHS
Weaknesses:
REV_WEAKNESSES
REV_PROSE_SECOND

Other comments:
REV_DETAILS
REV_CLOSING
}

REV_OPENING=text
REV_OPENING REV_OPEN_VERDICT REV_OPEN_FOLLOW

REV_PROSE_CONCERN=text
REV_PROSE_CONCERN REV_MAIN_CONCERN

REV_PROSE_SECOND=text
REV_PROSE_SECOND a note on presentation: the writing is REV_WRITING_BAD in places, particularly Section REV_SEC, and the figures would benefit from REV_FIG_FIX.
REV_PROSE_SECOND the paper is REV_WRITING overall. the main exception is Section REV_SEC, which REV_SEC_PROBLEM.
REV_PROSE_SECOND I also want to flag the related work: it is REV_RELWORK_BAD, and a reader unfamiliar with PAPER_THING would not learn from it how SYSNAME differs from REV_PRIOR_WORK.
REV_PROSE_SECOND on the evaluation more generally: I would have liked to see REV_EVAL_WISH. the current experiments answer the question of whether SYSNAME works, but not REV_EVAL_QUESTION.

REV_FIG_FIX error bars
REV_FIG_FIX axis labels
REV_FIG_FIX a consistent legend
REV_FIG_FIX larger fonts

REV_SEC_PROBLEM reads like a list of implementation details with no thread connecting them
REV_SEC_PROBLEM introduces SCI_THING without explaining why
REV_SEC_PROBLEM could be cut in half without losing anything
REV_SEC_PROBLEM contradicts the abstract on what SYSNAME actually does

REV_EVAL_WISH a sensitivity analysis on REV_NODES nodes
REV_EVAL_WISH a breakdown of where the time goes
REV_EVAL_WISH a comparison against SCI_THING
REV_EVAL_WISH an experiment with REV_STRESS

REV_EVAL_QUESTION why
REV_EVAL_QUESTION when it stops working
REV_EVAL_QUESTION whether the REV_BENEFIT comes from the design or from the implementation

REV_Q_ITEM=text
REV_Q_ITEM - REV_D_QUESTION
REV_D_QUESTION!

REV_CLOSING=text
REV_CLOSING REV_CLOSE_SENTENCE REV_CLOSE_EXTRA

